$DIR/main.p:4:9: runtime string concatenation is not permitted in Plutonium (it allocates); use pluto/strings.Builder
    Joining strings with + makes a new string, and that needs memory. Plutonium
    only allows + on strings whose values are constants, which the compiler
    joins while compiling.
    To build a string at run time, use pluto/strings.Builder, which takes an
    allocator, or format into a buffer of your own with fmt.Bprintf.
    In Go the result of + is allocated and later freed by the garbage collector.
    Plutonium has no garbage collector and never allocates behind the program's
    back.
    Use a Builder:
        var b strings.Builder
        defer b.Free()
        b.WriteString("hello, ")
        b.WriteString(name)
        greeting := b.String()
    (see "What Plutonium removes from Go" in the Plutonium spec, section 3)
    (run "pluto explain E2541" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:9:3: defer inside a loop is not supported in Plutonium (it would need unbounded stack); move the loop body into a function
    A defer statement inside a loop would add a deferred call on every
    iteration, and each needs room in the function's stack frame until it
    returns. Plutonium gives every deferred call a fixed slot in the frame, so
    the number must be known when compiling.
    Move the loop body into a function of its own; its defer then runs at the
    end of each iteration.
    Go stores such deferred calls on the heap. Plutonium never allocates behind
    the program's back, and kernel stacks are small and fixed.
    Move the body into a function:
        for _, name := range names {
            process(name)
        }

        func process(name string) {
            f := open(name)
            defer f.Close()
            ...
        }
    (see "Defer statements" in the Go spec; Plutonium adds this limit)
    (run "pluto explain E2530" for a longer explanation)
